# Toolchain: Node.js + pnpm + Vite + tsx

This project uses Node.js with pnpm workspaces. **Never introduce Bun, Deno, or other runtimes.**

## Package Manager
- Use `pnpm install` — never `bun install`, `npm install`, or `yarn install`
- Use `pnpm exec <tool>` to run local binaries
- Use `pnpm --filter @satyrsmc/<package> <script>` to run a script in a specific package
- Use `pnpm -r <script>` to run a script across all packages

## Running Scripts/Files
- Use `tsx <file>` for one-off TypeScript scripts — never `bun <file>` or `node <file>` directly
- Scripts that need `.env` must start with `import "dotenv/config"` — Node.js does not auto-load `.env`

## Testing
- Use `vitest` for all tests — never `bun test` or `jest`
- Run tests: `pnpm test` (all packages) or `pnpm --filter @satyrsmc/<package> test`

## Frontend Bundling (app-public, app-members)
- Use Vite for dev server and production builds
- Dev: `pnpm --filter @satyrsmc/app-public dev` (Vite on port 5173)
- Build: `pnpm --filter @satyrsmc/app-public build`
- Use `@tailwindcss/vite` plugin — never `bun-plugin-tailwind`

## API Bundling
- Use `tsx` for local development (no bundler): `pnpm --filter @satyrsmc/api dev`
- Use `tsup` for production Netlify Function bundle only: `pnpm --filter @satyrsmc/api build:function`
- **No Vite in the API package** — only tsup for production output

## HTTP Server
- Use Express — never `Bun.serve()`
- Use `node:fs` (`existsSync`, `readFileSync`, etc.) — never `Bun.file()`
- Cookie manipulation uses `res.append("Set-Cookie", ...)` — not `Headers.append()`

## Environment Variables
- Node.js does NOT auto-load `.env` — always add `import "dotenv/config"` as the first import in server entry points and scripts
- Never rely on Bun's automatic `.env` loading
